"eng": €€€€
"Large language models can generate fluent text but cannot reliably answer questions whose answer lies outside their training data, which has made retrieval-augmented generation a standard architecture for grounded question answering over private corpora. Vector retrieval is the cheap default, but it can fail when the answer requires combining information from several documents. Graph-enhanced retrieval methods such as GraphRAG and LightRAG address this limitation by constructing a graph of entities and relations over the corpus, at the price of being significantly more compute-intensive than vector retrieval. Applying graph-enhanced retrieval to every query is therefore wasteful when the cheap mode already suffices.

This thesis studies whether the choice between LightRAG's cheap vector retrieval mode (\emph{Naive}) and its more expensive graph-enhanced mode (\emph{Mix}) can be predicted from the query text alone, using a lightweight encoder-only classifier as the routing layer rather than a large language-model controller. Routing labels are constructed on the 2WikiMultihopQA benchmark by executing both retrieval modes on each question and using an LLM-as-a-Judge protocol to compare each mode's answer against the gold answer. A ModernBERT-based router is compared against a TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline as well as static always-\emph{Naive}, always-\emph{Mix}, and majority-class reference policies, and is reported with 1\,000-iteration bootstrap confidence intervals across three training seeds.

On the held-out test split, the ModernBERT router reaches an AUPRC of $0.732 \pm 0.005$ on the minority \texttt{mix\_required} class, a balanced accuracy of $0.756 \pm 0.008$, and an F1 of $0.645 \pm 0.002$, improving on a strong TF-IDF baseline (AUPRC 0.708, balanced accuracy 0.727, F1 0.605) while remaining within bootstrap intervals on AUROC. A per-question-type breakdown localizes most of the routing signal in the compositional question slice, where the encoder router improves AUPRC by roughly six points over the TF-IDF baseline. At its validation-selected operating point the router reduces mean routed execution time by roughly one third compared with always-\emph{Mix} while keeping non-trivial recall on the minority class. The result indicates that retrieval-mode selection in a Hybrid retrieval-augmented generation system can be made adaptive without moving the routing decision itself into an expensive model call."
